e to  measure our progress on reducing it so I  would treat all numbers as  approximations  but most of the time we say that about  one-third of the food that we produce is  lost or wasted and this recent unept  report actually suggests that the  problem may be even larger than that so  just terminology we we generally use the  term food loss to refer to food that is  lost in the production in the processing  stages before it ever reaches a retail  outlet or the consumer so food that was  damaged in the field or food that was  simply not harvested because of cosmetic  standards for some other reason or food  that's spoiled in storage or on the way  from the farm to the processing plant  that would be food loss and that's a  major problem in poor countries in  particular because of a lack of reliable  transportation lack of  climate-controlled storage facilities we  use the term food waste and that's the  subject of this of this unep report to  refer to food that is not sold or eaten  at the retail of a food service or the  consumer end of the of the supply chain  so we used to think that food waste was  more prevalent in wealthy countries than  in poor countries this unep court report  actually challenges that idea one of the  key findings from the report is that  household food waste is a problem across  income levels  so it's problem in poorer countries just  as it is in wealthy ones and we need to  pay attention to it everywhere another  Finding from this report is that food  waste in households may be twice as high  as we previously thought  so the graphic on the left shows that  households are the source of most food  waste followed by food service and in  retail and the graphic on the right  breaks this down by country so countries  with large populations like China and  India understandably produce in net  terms the most food waste and that's  what the orange bars there depict but  the yellow circles on the right show  food waste per capita right and the  pic